 "post": {
  "title": "I Bet Tails After 6 Heads in a Row #shorts",
  "description": "Would you have bet tails? Tell me in the comments. Send this to the friend who's always 'due'. Six heads in a row and tails feels certain, but a fair coin starts fresh every flip: fifty-fifty, every time. In 1913 at the Monte Carlo Casino the roulette ball landed on black 26 times in a row while gamblers kept betting against it. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman ('Belief in the law of small numbers', Psychological Bulletin, 1971) showed that people expect even short random sequences to look balanced: the gambler's fallacy. #psychology #shorts #gamblersfallacy #overthinking #luck",
  "tags": [
   "gambler's fallacy",
   "law of small numbers",
   "Tversky Kahneman",
   "coin flip",
   "psychology"
  ]
 },
